---
import SiteLayout from "../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import FlavorTabs from "../../../components/FlavorTabs.astro";
import ModuleHero from "../../../components/module/ModuleHero.astro";
import ModuleScenario from "../../../components/module/ModuleScenario.astro";
import ModuleFlowDiagram from "../../../components/module/ModuleFlowDiagram.astro";
import IdempotentIngestDemo from "../../../components/module/demos/IdempotentIngestDemo.astro";
import ModuleFooterNav from "../../../components/module/ModuleFooterNav.astro";
import ModuleRecap from "../../../components/module/ModuleRecap.astro";
import ModuleQuiz from "../../../components/module/ModuleQuiz.astro";
import { flavor as gcp } from "../../../data/flavors/gcp";
import { flavor as aws } from "../../../data/flavors/aws";
import { flavor as azure } from "../../../data/flavors/azure";
import { flavor as oss } from "../../../data/flavors/oss";
import { MODULES } from "../../../data/modules";

const moduleId = "10-ingestion";
const entries = {
  gcp: gcp[moduleId],
  aws: aws[moduleId],
  azure: azure[moduleId],
  oss: oss[moduleId],
};
const moduleData = MODULES.find((m) => m.id === moduleId)!;
---

<SiteLayout title="10 · Ingestion" description={moduleData.summary}>
  <article>
    <ModuleHero moduleId={moduleId}>
      <p>
        Every pipeline starts the same way regardless of cloud: get raw content — documents, tickets, chats, web
        pages — into durable object storage, then register enough metadata (source, filename, ingested-at,
        checksum) in a table so later stages can find it and avoid re-processing duplicates.
      </p>
    </ModuleHero>

    <ModuleScenario moduleId={moduleId} />

    <ModuleFlowDiagram moduleId={moduleId} />

    <h2>Why object storage first, not straight to a vector DB</h2>
    <p>
      Parsing, OCR, and chunking strategies change over time — you will re-run them. Keeping the raw file in cheap
      object storage keeps every downstream stage replayable from source, instead of a one-way trip into an index
      you can't reconstruct.
    </p>

    <h2>Idempotency</h2>
    <p>
      Re-running an ingestion job on the same source must not create duplicate rows. Minimal pattern: hash the raw
      file, use that hash (or a stable source ID) as part of the storage key, and let a duplicate write short-circuit
      as "already ingested" instead of doubling your document count.
    </p>

    <IdempotentIngestDemo />

    <h2>Partial-batch failures need per-item status, not job-level retries</h2>
    <p>
      Idempotency solves "the whole job crashed and reran." It doesn't solve "480 of 500 files ingested, then the
      481st threw a parsing error." Retrying the whole batch redoes the 480 that succeeded; skipping the batch
      leaves all 480 good documents un-ingested alongside the 20 bad ones.
    </p>
    <p>The fix: add a per-document status column to the same metadata table, not just a row that exists or doesn't.</p>
    <div class="concept-flow" role="list" aria-label="Per-document retry status flow">
      <span class="chip chip--accent" role="listitem">PENDING</span>
      <span class="concept-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <span class="chip" role="listitem">DONE or FAILED</span>
      <span class="concept-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <span class="chip" role="listitem">RETRY re-runs PENDING/FAILED only</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      This is naturally idempotent — succeeded rows are already <code>done</code> and get skipped, not reprocessed.
    </p>

    <h2>Data quality and PII redaction before embedding</h2>
    <p>
      Everything above assumes the raw file is safe to store and eventually embed. It often isn't. <strong>Schema validation</strong> at ingestion time — does this record have the fields and types downstream
      stages expect? — catches malformed data before parsing. <strong>PII detection and redaction</strong> —
      scanning for emails, phone numbers, national IDs, and similar patterns — has to happen here too, before a
      customer's SSN ends up embedded and retrievable by anyone who asks the right question.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fixing this here is cheap: a redaction rule runs once per document. Leaving the gap open means every
      embedding, index entry, and cached response downstream carries the same exposed PII, and fixing it later
      means re-ingesting, re-embedding, and re-indexing everything.
    </p>

    <h2>Try it in your flavor</h2>
    <FlavorTabs moduleId={moduleId} entries={entries} />

    <h2>Pitfalls</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Skipping metadata (source, timestamp, checksum) — you'll need it for dedup, ACLs, and debugging retrieval later.</li>
      <li>Ingesting straight into a vector index with no raw-file copy — you lose the ability to re-chunk or re-embed later.</li>
      <li>No idempotency check — re-running a job during development silently multiplies your document count.</li>
      <li>Skipping PII redaction at ingestion because "we'll clean it up later" — later means re-embedding and re-indexing every document, not editing one table.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
  <ModuleQuiz moduleId={moduleId} />
  <ModuleRecap moduleId={moduleId} />
  <ModuleFooterNav moduleId={moduleId} />
</SiteLayout>
